# When an alert fires

When a rule's condition becomes true, Nocturne opens an alert. This page
covers what happens next: who is told, how the alert ends, and what
acknowledging, snoozing and Do Not Disturb each do.

## What happens straight away

1. The alert is recorded in **Alerts > History**, with the time it started.
2. Unless Do Not Disturb holds it back, it is sent to **every channel** on the
   rule at once.
3. In the Nocturne app, it shows as an active alert until it ends.

Nocturne sends the alert **once**, when it fires. It does not repeat it on its
own while the alert stays open. It sends it again only when a snooze runs out
and the alert is still there. If you want someone else told when nobody
responds, build that as its own rule; see
[Example rules](/docs/alerts/recipes#tell-someone-else-if-nobody-responds).

## How an alert ends

An alert ends in one of four ways:

| How | When |
|-----|------|
| **The condition stops being true** | At the next check where the condition is false: the next reading for glucose rules, or within 30 seconds for rules about time passing, such as signal loss. |
| **Auto-resolve** | The rule's auto-resolve condition becomes true. See below. |
| **Closed by hand** | Someone closes it from the app. |
| **The rule is switched off** | Switching a rule off closes its open alert. |

Once it has ended, the rule can fire again, and the next time is a new alert,
sent again to every channel. A glucose level that hovers on the line can
therefore alert, end and alert again a few readings apart. If that happens, a
"for at least" setting on the rule (see
[Conditions that must last](/docs/alerts/sustained)) is the usual fix.

## Auto-resolve

An alert already ends when its condition stops being true. Auto-resolve is for
ending it **sooner**, while the condition still holds, because something else
has happened that makes the alert no longer useful.

Here are two high alerts, above 250 mg/dL (13.9 mmol/L), after a meal. The
second has an auto-resolve condition: glucose falling at least 1 mg/dL (0.06
mmol/L) a minute for at least 15 minutes.

<AlertSimulator scenario="meal-high" rules={["high", "high-auto-resolve"]} />

"High" stays on until glucose drops under 250. "High, until it is coming down"
ends 15 minutes after the fall begins, while glucose is still above 250.

After an auto-resolve, the rule waits before it can fire again: it stays quiet
while its condition **and** the auto-resolve condition are both still true.
As soon as either stops being true, the rule is back to normal. So if glucose
stopped falling while still above 250, the auto-resolve condition would no
longer hold, and the rule would alert again straight away. It does not wait for
glucose to go back below 250 first.

<Callout type="warning" title="Think about what auto-resolve hides">

  Auto-resolve ends an alert while the thing it warns about is still true.
  Choose an auto-resolve condition that really means the situation is being
  dealt with, and test it with Replay (see
  [Testing rules](/docs/alerts/testing)) before relying on it.

</Callout>

## Acknowledging

When an alert card appears in the app, it offers:

- **Snooze** for 5, 15, 30 or 60 minutes, covered below.
- **Acknowledge**, which records that someone has seen the alert. It does
  **not** end the alert: the alert stays open until its condition stops being
  true. Acknowledging does stop rules that watch for "this alert is still
  unacknowledged", which is how escalation to a second person is stopped.
- **Mute for me** appears instead of Acknowledge for someone who does not have
  permission to manage alerts, such as a family member with view access. It stops
  the alert notifying **them**, and changes nothing for anyone else.
- A button to open the rule's settings.
- **X**, which only closes the card on this screen. It records nothing.

## Snoozing

A snooze silences an alert on every channel for the time you choose. The alert
stays open. When the snooze runs out, if the alert is still there, it has not
been acknowledged, and Do Not Disturb does not cover it, it is sent again.

- An alert can be snoozed **3 times** by default. After that, snoozing it is
  refused until the alert ends.
- A snooze is not an acknowledgement. A rule watching for "unacknowledged" still
  sees a snoozed alert as unacknowledged, so escalation carries on.

### Smart snooze

With **Smart snooze** switched on in the rule, a snooze that runs out is
extended by a few minutes at a time (10 by default, set with **Extend by**)
while the conditions you give under **Extend while** all hold. The usual use is
a low alert: you have eaten something, and you do not want to be told again
while glucose is climbing back.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-smart-snooze" />

- Each extension counts towards the snooze limit, the same as snoozing by hand.
- The conditions only use a glucose reading from the last 11 minutes. With no
  reading that recent, glucose conditions are not true, the snooze is not
  extended, and the alert is sent again.
- With smart snooze on and **no** conditions, Nocturne only extends snoozes on
  rules that compare glucose with a value, and only while glucose is clearly
  heading back: for a low alert, up more than 4 mg/dL (0.2 mmol/L) in 5 minutes
  or more than 10 mg/dL (0.6 mmol/L) in 10 minutes; for a high alert, down more
  than 1 mg/dL in 5 minutes or more than 2 mg/dL in 10 minutes. Every other
  alert is sent again when its snooze ends.

## Do Not Disturb

Do Not Disturb holds back alerts for a while. Open it from **Alerts > Do Not
Disturb**.

<Screenshot id="alerts-dnd" />

- **Manual** turns it on now, optionally until a time you choose.
- **Schedule** turns it on every day between two times: quiet hours. The times
  use the time zone on your patient record, and can cross midnight.

While Do Not Disturb is on:

- **Critical** rules still reach you, always.
- Rules with **Allow through Do Not Disturb** ticked still reach you.
- Every other alert still **fires** and is recorded in history as silenced by
  Do Not Disturb. It is just not sent.

Some apps that connect to Nocturne can also hold back only low alerts, or only
high alerts, for a while. A rule that watches for both lows and highs, or for
something that is neither, is only held back by Do Not Disturb for everything.

<Callout type="warning" title="Quiet hours are when lows happen">

  Overnight is when people are least likely to notice a low on their own. If you
  use quiet hours, make sure the rules that matter most at night are Critical, or
  have **Allow through Do Not Disturb** ticked.

</Callout>

## History

**Alerts > History** lists every alert that has fired: which rule, how serious
it was, when it started and ended, how long it lasted, and whether someone
acknowledged it. Test alerts are not listed.

<Screenshot id="alerts-history" />
